WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.100 --> 00:00:01.360
How do we prove a

2
00:00:01.360 --> 00:00:04.280
recurrence bound by substitution? We

3
00:00:04.280 --> 00:00:06.380
will guess a concrete inequality,

4
00:00:07.040 --> 00:00:08.760
verify its base case, and

5
00:00:08.760 --> 00:00:09.980
carry it through an induction

6
00:00:09.980 --> 00:00:11.780
step without assuming the conclusion.

7
00:00:12.300 --> 00:00:14.280
Our example has T of

8
00:00:14.280 --> 00:00:16.140
one equal to zero and

9
00:00:16.140 --> 00:00:17.480
T of n equal to

10
00:00:17.480 --> 00:00:18.860
T of n minus one

11
00:00:18.860 --> 00:00:22.000
plus n minus one. Expanding

12
00:00:22.000 --> 00:00:23.960
a few terms suggests quadratic

13
00:00:23.960 --> 00:00:25.960
growth, but we must still

14
00:00:25.960 --> 00:00:27.780
justify that quadratic guess.

15
00:00:28.660 --> 00:00:30.640
We choose the concrete upper

16
00:00:30.640 --> 00:00:32.420
bound T of n at

17
00:00:32.420 --> 00:00:35.100
most n squared. This fixes

18
00:00:35.100 --> 00:00:37.280
the multiplicative constant at one;

19
00:00:37.900 --> 00:00:39.960
if that attempt failed, we

20
00:00:39.960 --> 00:00:41.380
would need a different constant

21
00:00:41.380 --> 00:00:43.800
or a stronger candidate inequality.

22
00:00:44.460 --> 00:00:45.900
Begin at n equal to

23
00:00:45.900 --> 00:00:48.080
one, where the actual recurrence

24
00:00:48.080 --> 00:00:50.080
gives zero. Since zero is

25
00:00:50.080 --> 00:00:51.420
at most one squared, the

26
00:00:51.420 --> 00:00:52.180
base case holds.

27
00:00:52.740 --> 00:00:54.860
Now assume the bound holds

28
00:00:54.860 --> 00:00:57.200
at k, namely T of

29
00:00:57.200 --> 00:00:58.840
k at most k squared.

30
00:00:59.500 --> 00:01:01.220
This is our induction hypothesis

31
00:01:01.220 --> 00:01:03.180
about the smaller input, not

32
00:01:03.180 --> 00:01:04.320
an assumption that the desired

33
00:01:04.320 --> 00:01:05.800
bound already holds at k

34
00:01:05.800 --> 00:01:06.300
plus one.

35
00:01:07.020 --> 00:01:09.660
Apply the recurrence first: T

36
00:01:09.660 --> 00:01:11.280
of k plus one equals

37
00:01:11.280 --> 00:01:13.360
T of k plus k.

38
00:01:13.980 --> 00:01:15.940
Substituting the hypothesis gives at

39
00:01:15.940 --> 00:01:17.860
most k squared plus k,

40
00:01:18.220 --> 00:01:19.860
which is at most k

41
00:01:19.860 --> 00:01:21.480
squared plus two k plus

42
00:01:21.480 --> 00:01:21.820
one.

43
00:01:22.340 --> 00:01:24.360
That closes the step, and

44
00:01:24.360 --> 00:01:26.500
induction proves the claimed upper

45
00:01:26.500 --> 00:01:28.420
bound for all positive integer

46
00:01:28.420 --> 00:01:30.380
n. Therefore the function is

47
00:01:30.380 --> 00:01:32.100
big O of n squared,

48
00:01:32.580 --> 00:01:34.120
but this proof alone has

49
00:01:34.120 --> 00:01:35.800
not supplied a matching lower

50
00:01:35.800 --> 00:01:36.100
bound.

51
00:01:36.460 --> 00:01:38.000
To make the result tight,

52
00:01:38.480 --> 00:01:41.080
expand the recurrence completely. It

53
00:01:41.080 --> 00:01:42.680
sums one through n minus

54
00:01:42.680 --> 00:01:44.760
one, giving n times n

55
00:01:44.760 --> 00:01:46.520
minus one over two and

56
00:01:46.520 --> 00:01:48.220
therefore Theta of n squared.

57
00:01:48.660 --> 00:01:50.820
For a divide-by-two recurrence, it

58
00:01:50.820 --> 00:01:52.080
can be easier to induct

59
00:01:52.080 --> 00:01:54.300
over powers of two. Assume

60
00:01:54.300 --> 00:01:55.640
the statement for input two

61
00:01:55.640 --> 00:01:56.680
to the k and use

62
00:01:56.680 --> 00:01:57.680
it to prove the statement

63
00:01:57.680 --> 00:01:58.880
for input two to the

64
00:01:58.880 --> 00:01:59.840
k plus one.

65
00:02:01.020 --> 00:02:02.920
Consider T of n equals

66
00:02:02.920 --> 00:02:04.760
two T of n over

67
00:02:04.760 --> 00:02:06.660
two plus n minus one,

68
00:02:07.060 --> 00:02:08.440
with T of one equal

69
00:02:08.440 --> 00:02:10.160
to one. The tempting inequality

70
00:02:10.160 --> 00:02:11.600
T of n at most

71
00:02:11.600 --> 00:02:13.460
c n log base two

72
00:02:13.460 --> 00:02:14.920
of n fails at n

73
00:02:14.920 --> 00:02:16.900
equal to one, because its

74
00:02:16.900 --> 00:02:17.980
right side is zero.

75
00:02:18.420 --> 00:02:20.000
We can instead start the

76
00:02:20.000 --> 00:02:22.380
power-of-two induction at n equal

77
00:02:22.380 --> 00:02:24.060
to two, where T of

78
00:02:24.060 --> 00:02:26.480
two equals three. Choosing c

79
00:02:26.480 --> 00:02:27.940
at least two makes the

80
00:02:27.940 --> 00:02:29.880
base hold, and substituting the

81
00:02:29.880 --> 00:02:32.020
hypothesis yields c n log

82
00:02:32.020 --> 00:02:33.880
n minus c n plus

83
00:02:33.880 --> 00:02:35.940
n minus one, which stays

84
00:02:35.940 --> 00:02:37.540
below c n log n.

85
00:02:37.820 --> 00:02:39.840
A different valid repair is

86
00:02:39.840 --> 00:02:41.440
to strengthen the candidate bound

87
00:02:41.440 --> 00:02:43.120
by adding a linear term.

88
00:02:43.740 --> 00:02:45.640
Whatever repair you choose, it

89
00:02:45.640 --> 00:02:46.900
must be checked against the

90
00:02:46.900 --> 00:02:48.720
recurrence and the actual domain

91
00:02:48.720 --> 00:02:50.720
rather than being waved away

92
00:02:50.720 --> 00:02:52.180
as a small-input detail.

93
00:02:52.900 --> 00:02:54.240
Things to take note of:

94
00:02:54.240 --> 00:02:56.100
distinguish the guess from its

95
00:02:56.100 --> 00:02:58.640
proof, fix your constants, and

96
00:02:58.640 --> 00:03:01.060
substitute only a hypothesis established

97
00:03:01.060 --> 00:03:03.420
for smaller inputs. Check the

98
00:03:03.420 --> 00:03:04.780
base and state whether you

99
00:03:04.780 --> 00:03:06.700
proved O or Theta; the

100
00:03:06.700 --> 00:03:08.100
missing half of a tight-bound

101
00:03:08.100 --> 00:03:09.780
argument will not prove itself,

102
00:03:09.780 --> 00:03:10.400
obviously.
